workedExample:
Go character by character:

```
<   -> &lt;
b   -> b
>   -> &gt;
Tom -> Tom
&   -> &amp;
...
```

Result: `&lt;b&gt;Tom &amp; Jerry&lt;/b&gt;`. The browser renders it as the visible text `<b>Tom & Jerry</b>`, not bold. That's the whole point: data stays data.

prompt:
This SPA code is vulnerable to DOM XSS:

```javascript
const t = decodeURIComponent(
  location.hash.slice(1));
box.innerHTML = t;
```

The team adds HTML escaping to every server response. Does that fix it?

workedExample:
1. Without Trusted Types: every `innerHTML = s` is a potential DOM XSS; you must audit hundreds of sites.
2. With enforcement: `el.innerHTML = "<b>x</b>"` throws a TypeError.
3. You create a policy:

```javascript
const p = trustedTypes.createPolicy(
  "app", {createHTML: s =>
    DOMPurify.sanitize(s)});
el.innerHTML = p.createHTML(s);
```

4. Now the only code that can turn a string into HTML is the policy, so that's what you audit. Roll out in report-only mode first to find the violations.
